{
    "id": "year7-english-modals-lesson-3-obligation",
    "title": "Expressing Obligation, Necessity & Permission",
    "description": "Distinguish between strict mandates, advisory recommendations, and formal permission using modal verbs like must, have to, should, ought to, can, and may.",
    "duration": 30,
    "level": "Year 7",
    "slides": [
        {
            "id": "slide-1",
            "title": "Mandates vs. Advice",
            "subtitle": "Understanding Rules and Recommendations",
            "layout": "hero",
            "components": [
                {
                    "id": "obligation-intro-heading",
                    "type": "heading",
                    "props": {
                        "content": "Strict Obligation vs. Advisory Advice",
                        "level": 2,
                        "align": "left"
                    }
                },
                {
                    "id": "obligation-intro-paragraph",
                    "type": "paragraph",
                    "props": {
                        "content": "Modal verbs indicate how compulsory an action is. A **strict mandate** leaves no choice, whereas an **advisory recommendation** offers strong guidance while allowing individual choice.",
                        "align": "left"
                    }
                },
                {
                    "id": "obligation-summary-table",
                    "type": "table",
                    "props": {
                        "rows": 4,
                        "columns": 3,
                        "data": [
                            [
                                "Category",
                                "Modal Verbs Used",
                                "Example Sentence"
                            ],
                            [
                                "Strict Mandate (No Choice)",
                                "Must / Have to",
                                "Drivers **must** stop at red traffic lights."
                            ],
                            [
                                "Strong Recommendation",
                                "Should / Ought to",
                                "You **should** drink water during long workouts."
                            ],
                            [
                                "Prohibition (Forbidden)",
                                "Must not / Cannot",
                                "You **must not** enter without a permit."
                            ]
                        ]
                    }
                }
            ]
        },
        {
            "id": "slide-2",
            "title": "Permission & Ability: May vs. Can",
            "subtitle": "Navigating Formal Rules and Personal Capacity",
            "layout": "split",
            "components": [
                {
                    "id": "permission-heading",
                    "type": "heading",
                    "props": {
                        "content": "Formal Permission vs. Physical Ability",
                        "level": 3,
                        "align": "left"
                    }
                },
                {
                    "id": "permission-paragraph",
                    "type": "paragraph",
                    "props": {
                        "content": "In formal English, **may** grants or requests official permission ('May I ask a question?'), while **can** describes physical capability ('She can run ten miles'). Using 'may' projects courtesy and respect.",
                        "align": "left"
                    }
                },
                {
